/-!
# The second spectral sequence of Tag 0BLX at an ideal sheaf generated by global sections

Let `A` be a ring, `I ⊂ A` a finitely generated ideal, `(𝒞, 𝒪)` a ringed site with `𝒪` a sheaf
of `A`-algebras and `ℱ` a sheaf of `𝒪`-modules. The second spectral sequence of
[Stacks, Tag 0BLX] is
```
E₂^{p,q} = H^p(𝒞, H^q(ℱ^∧))   ⟹   H^{p+q}(RΓ(𝒞, ℱ^∧)),
```
the completion being taken along the ideal sheaf `I𝒪`.

`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_pages_derivedCompletion_globalSections` of
`GlobalSectionsCompletion.lean` proves it from two inputs it has to *assume*, and therefore
carries no tag:

1. [Stacks, Tag 099F], the existence of the derived completion functor on `D(𝒪)`, as the
   instance argument `[(ObjectProperty.ι (IsDerivedComplete X 𝓘)).IsRightAdjoint]` — without it
   `ℱ^∧` cannot be written down;
2. a **bounded below representative** of `ℱ^∧`, as a bounded below cochain complex `K` together
   with an isomorphism `Q(K) ≅ ℱ^∧` — without it the spectral sequence of [Stacks, Tag 015J],
   which is a `D⁺` statement, does not apply.

This file discharges **both**, for the sheaf of ideals `ℐ = (f_1, …, f_r)` generated by finitely
many global sections of `𝒪`, and registers the resulting statement.

That is no loss of generality. `I` finitely generated is a hypothesis of the tag, so
`I = (a_1, …, a_r)`, and `𝒪` being a sheaf of `A`-algebras the global sections `ρ a_1, …, ρ a_r`
of `𝒪` generate `I𝒪`; since `I𝒪` is unique, *every* ideal sheaf the tag completes along is an
`ℐ = (f_1, …, f_r)`. That is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_eq_generatedIdealSheaf_of_isIdealSheafGeneratedBy` of
`GeneratedIdealSheaf.lean`, and it is why the statement registered below is at the tag's own
generality rather than in a restricted regime.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.bijective_precomp_derivedCompletionUnit_app`: the universal
  property of the reflector of [Stacks, Tag 099F] in elementary form — precomposition with
  `K ⟶ K^∧` is a bijection onto the morphisms of `K` into a derived-complete object.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_iso_derivedCompletion_completionFunctor_obj`: at
  `ℐ = (f_1, …, f_r)` the reflector **is** the Čech completion of [Stacks, Tag 099B],
  objectwise: `K^∧ ≅ R𝑆heafHom_𝒪(Č(f), K)`, by an isomorphism carrying the reflector's
  completion map to the Čech one.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isLE_derivedCategoryQ_extendedAlternatingCech`: `Č(f)` is
  cohomologically bounded above by `r`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isGE_derivedCompletion_generatedIdealSheaf_obj`: hence `K^∧` is
  cohomologically bounded below by `b - r` whenever `K` is bounded below by `b`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_plus_derivedCompletion_generatedIdealSheaf_obj`: hence
  `K^∧` has a bounded below representative — the second assumed input, proved.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_pages_derivedCompletion_globalSections_generatedIdealSheaf`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 0BLX], the second spectral sequence**, with both inputs discharged.

## The argument

The two discharges are independent of each other, and the second rests on the first.

*Tag 099F.* At `ℐ = (f_1, …, f_r)` it is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf` of
`GeneratedIdealAdjoint.lean`, proved from the ambient ringed site and the generators alone. It is
a local instance here, so no statement below carries it.

*Boundedness.* The reflector is identified with the Čech completion
`K ↦ R𝑆heafHom_𝒪(Č(f), K)` by the uniqueness of reflections: both are derived complete, and
precomposition with each of the two completion maps is a bijection onto the morphisms out of `K`
into a derived-complete object — `bijective_precomp_derivedCompletionUnit_app` for the reflector
and `bijective_precomp_completionUnit_generatedIdealSheaf` of `GeneratedIdealAdjoint.lean` for
the Čech functor — so each factors through the other and the two factorizations compose to the
identity.

The Čech complex vanishes above degree `r` (`isZero_extendedAlternatingCech_X_of_lt`), so it is
cohomologically `≤ r`; a sheaf in degree zero is cohomologically `≥ 0`; and the derived internal
Hom of a cohomologically `≤ d` object into a cohomologically `≥ b` one is cohomologically
`≥ b - d`, which is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isGE_derivedSheafHom` of
`StacksFrontier/CategoryTheory/Sites/Cohomology/InternalHom/DerivedHomBoundedBelow.lean`. So
`ℱ^∧`
is cohomologically `≥ -r`, and `DerivedCategory.exists_iso_Q_obj_of_isGE` turns that into an
honest bounded below complex together with the isomorphism. Nothing about `ℱ` is used beyond its
being a sheaf.

## What is covered of [Stacks, Tag 0BLX], and what is not

The registered statement below is the tag's **second spectral sequence**, at a sheaf of ideals
generated by finitely many global sections of `𝒪`. Its hypotheses are the source's own — `I`
finitely generated, `𝓘 = I𝒪` — and nothing else: no conclusion of the tag, and no conclusion of
a tag the tag's proof invokes, is assumed, and by the paragraph above the generators are a
presentation of the source's `I𝒪` rather than a condition on it.

Two other parts of the tag are registered elsewhere, and are untouched here: the first spectral
sequence, at the source's own generality
(`exists_spectralSequence_derivedCompletion_globalSections` of `GlobalSectionsCompletion.lean`),
and the identity `RΓ(𝒞, ℱ)^∧ = RΓ(𝒞, ℱ^∧)`, at that same generality
(`nonempty_iso_derivedCompletion_derivedGlobalSections` of
`GlobalSectionsCompletionKoszulIdentity.lean`), and in the nilpotent regime
(`exists_iso_derivedCompletion_derivedGlobalSections` of `UnboundedGlobalSections.lean`). All
three assertions together are
`exists_iso_and_pages_derivedCompletion_globalSections` of `GlobalSectionsCompletionWhole.lean`.

**The identity is not proved by the source's route.** The source proves it in a line, by
applying [Stacks, Tag 0A0G] to the morphism of ringed topoi `(𝒞, 𝒪) ⟶ (pt, A)`. Of Tag 0A0G this
library has the comparison map and two criteria for its invertibility — now unconditionally at a
generated ideal sheaf, in `PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean` — but **not** the
invertibility for an arbitrary object, which is that tag's whole assertion; and there is no
morphism of ringed sites or ringed topoi from a general ringed site to the ringed point, so that
route is unavailable even once the invertibility is proved. This is set out in the "The other two
assertions of Tag 0BLX" section of `GlobalSectionsCompletion.lean`. What the Koszul route
substitutes for it is the presentation of both completions as derived limits of one and the same
Koszul-Hom tower.

The second spectral sequence at an ideal sheaf of finite type that is *not* an `I𝒪` is not part
of the tag: the tag completes only along `I𝒪` for a finitely generated `I ⊂ A`, and such an `I𝒪`
is globally generated. It would be a genuine generalization, and it is blocked rather than
unfinished — [Stacks, Tag 099F] at a merely finite-type ideal sheaf needs one global object
`K ⟶ 𝒪` of [Stacks, Tag 099E], that object is not constructed in this library, the
interface its construction needs (`Stacks.LocalCohomology.FunctorialCechData`) has no inhabitant,
and the obvious shortcut is refuted by
`Stacks.FunctorialCechFamily.isEmpty_functorialGenerators`.

## Implementation notes

*Why a separate module.* `GlobalSectionsCompletion.lean`, where the engine lives, sits below the
whole Čech development in the import order — `GeneratedIdealSheaf.lean` imports it — so the
discharge cannot be written beside the engine.

*The Cartan-Eilenberg resolution.* It is universally quantified inside the existential rather
than existentially quantified, exactly as in the engine and in [Stacks, Tag 015J] itself, whose
statement takes one; its existence is [Stacks, Tag 015I],
`CochainComplex.exists_cartanEilenbergResolution`, available because `Mod(𝒪)` has enough
injectives. The `HomologicalComplex₂.HasTotal` instance argument is likewise the engine's and is
a completeness condition on `ModuleCat A`, not a mathematical hypothesis.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 015I](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/015I)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 015J](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/015J)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099B](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099B)
